<BaseLayout title="About Matthew Cochran — Product & Engineering Leadership" description="Matthew Cochran is a hands-on product and engineering leader specializing in dependable AI systems, technical turnarounds, and consequential product development.">
  <PageHeader eyebrow="About / Operating perspective" title="Build the product. Lead the system around it." description="Matthew Cochran is a player-coach product and engineering leader with more than twenty-five years across strategy, architecture, delivery, operations, and hands-on software construction." aside="Public company · Billion-dollar enterprise · Early-stage teams" />
  <section class="section shell about-manifesto">
    <div class="manifesto-lead reveal"><p class="kicker">The through-line</p><h2>Judgment becomes valuable when it survives contact with implementation.</h2></div>
    <div class="manifesto-copy reveal"><p>Matthew has served as CTO of publicly traded Artnet, Founder/CTO of TruthShield, and a hands-on architecture and engineering leader at Medidata, Shutterstock, and other product organizations. At Medidata, he represented flagship products in architecture governance, architected brownfield modernization, and led teams responsible for product repair and delivery.</p><p>His recent work spans quantum-secure cryptography, military intelligence, health technology, fintech, and governed AI. That breadth is not a collection of labels. It is repeated exposure to systems where correctness, security, evidence, and delivery discipline materially matter.</p><p>He remains hands-on: writing software, designing architectures, creating product and project systems, and building open-source tools that make AI-assisted engineering more deterministic and accountable.</p></div>
  </section>
  <section class="values-band">
    <div class="shell values-grid">
      <article><span>01</span><h3>Product judgment</h3><p>Clarify the customer, consequence, and commercial premise before optimizing output.</p></article>
      <article><span>02</span><h3>Delivery control</h3><p>Model dependencies, critical paths, decision rights, and evidence so complex work remains steerable.</p></article>
      <article><span>03</span><h3>Engineering depth</h3><p>Use explicit boundaries, threat models, verification, and operational design to make trust concrete.</p></article>
      <article><span>04</span><h3>Leadership by participation</h3><p>Set direction, build teams, and enter the work wherever the system needs clarity.</p></article>
    </div>
  </section>
